  "detail": null,
  "summary": "Harold Vance (pt-0002, MRN 4410-22): I reviewed his medications against RBC-PHARM-07. One dose changed: gabapentin.\n\nValues I used:\n- **Age:** 82.\n- **Serum creatinine:** 1.21 mg/dL from 10/20. The 1.88 from 10/21 is marked entered-in-error (the specimen was mislabelled and belongs to another patient), so I didn't use it.\n- **Weight:** 143 lb (64.9 kg), weighed 10/20. I didn't use the 132 lb from 10/22 because it was his own estimate by phone, not a measured weight.\n\nThe pharmacist's 10/21 screen used the invalid 1.88 creatinine. That's why its numbers (eGFR 35, CrCl 28) and suggested doses don't apply.\n\n**Apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily:** no change. The policy's apixaban rules cover only non-valvular atrial fibrillation, and he has no AF diagnosis. He takes it to prevent another clot after six months of treatment for his August 2025 PE, and hematology wrote that this dose is not adjusted for kidney function. The policy says not to change medications it doesn't cover.\n\n**Metformin 1000 mg twice daily:** no change. His eGFR is 59.8, which is 45 or above.\n\n**Gabapentin:** reduced from 600 mg three times daily (1800 mg/day) to 600 mg twice daily (1200 mg/day), using his current 600 mg tablet. His creatinine clearance is 43 mL/min, and the policy's range for 30–59 is 400–1400 mg/day given twice daily. I changed the existing order rather than writing a separate one; the new order is mr-t0001.\n\nHis other active medications aren't covered by the policy, so I left them alone. My progress note (doc-t0002) records the values, the result of each rule, the change and the cited results. No inbox item for this patient needed closing.\n\nHe hasn't been told about the gabapentin change yet. Someone should let him know and watch for his postherpetic neuralgia pain coming back.",
